**Blunt Verdict**
Your conclusion is partly right, but overbroad.

Correct: **single-source, single-registration seam routing cannot create a true ego-center view**. It can only choose which off-center camera owns each pixel.

Wrong/incomplete: **“mixing implies artifact” is false**. Naive alpha blending implies artifact. Production systems do not just average misaligned sources. Meta deghosts toward a source using flow magnitude; Jump composites splats in disparity order; Google does global smooth warp optimization. Your current view path still does `novel = warp_i*(1-a)+warp_j*a` after gating, so it is much closer to “alpha blend with abstain” than to Meta/Jump’s occlusion-aware compositing ([run_a1_streetview_pipeline.py](<D:/BaiduSyncdisk/2024 to future/koi chen/experiments/Waymo2Panorama/scripts/phase3/run_a1_streetview_pipeline.py:276>)). Meta’s `NovelView` explicitly describes flow-magnitude weighted deghosting, and the code prefers larger-flow contributions in the softmax ([NovelView.h](<D:/BaiduSyncdisk/2024 to future/koi chen/experiments/Waymo2Panorama/NovelView.h:47>), [NovelView.cpp](<D:/BaiduSyncdisk/2024 to future/koi chen/experiments/Waymo2Panorama/NovelView.cpp:78>)).

**What You Are Missing**
The missing piece is **layered visibility**, not more graphcut.

Google Street View’s public pipeline is “plausible seam repair”: dense optical-flow correspondences, then a spline-based globally regularized warp solved with Ceres. Google says the effect is intentionally subtle, and the objective is lining up overlaps, not rendering a physically correct single-center camera. That could make your L1 look cleaner, but it will not give faithful ego-center geometry.

Google’s later stitching paper is more relevant: it says a single registration fails with depth variation and proposes **multiple registrations**, MRF seam selection, and terms discouraging duplication/tearing; it explicitly says this moves toward layer-based stereo. That is the direct attack on your local optimum: your graphcut has one warped image per camera, not multiple depth hypotheses/layers.

Jump is even clearer: averaging splats ghosts; separate surfaces should be composited in disparity order with an over-operator, using interval-based compositing. That is the algorithmic family you should test, using your rig/LiDAR/flow as geometry evidence, not alpha blending.

**Can Near-Field Doubling Be Fixed By 2D?**
Yes, for co-visible Lambertian surfaces with good correspondence. Dense flow can warp both views to an intermediate ray and collapse duplication. Your gray-car zoom is exactly the case where exact Meta deghosting may help.

No, for occlusion and disocclusion. A near object at 3 m with 25 cm baseline moves about `2048/(2π)*0.25/3 ≈ 27 px`; at 10 m it is still about 8 px. If two cameras see different background around the object, a 2D method has no hidden surface to recover. It can choose one camera, warp one camera, blend two cameras, or hallucinate. True ego-center rendering needs depth/layers/visibility.

**3DGS + Diffusion?**
Do not use diffusion as the core solver for world-model training data. It will hide artifacts by rewriting evidence. Use it only for sky/top/bottom or tiny disocclusion holes with a generated-region mask.

The better path is **source-grounded single-center rendering** first: dense local MVS/flow-triangulated LDI or learned 3DGS, with LiDAR anchoring and source-fidelity masks. Your classical LiDAR DIBR script is the right class, but raw/IP-Basic LiDAR completion is too sparse/smeary for boundaries ([dibr_lidar_single_center.py](<D:/BaiduSyncdisk/2024 to future/koi chen/experiments/Waymo2Panorama/scripts/phase3/dibr_lidar_single_center.py:1>)). DrivingForward has the right representation class, but the screenshot is not deliverable quality yet.

**Single Decisive Next Experiment**
Run a one-seam **Jump-style LDI / disparity-ordered over-composite kill test** on the BMW/gray-car seam:

1. Use RAFT/GMFlow or plane-sweep MVS on the two raw adjacent cameras, constrained by known AV2 calibration.
2. Triangulate confident correspondences; fuse with LiDAR hits.
3. Render only that seam crop to the ego-center ERP with z-buffer / disparity-interval over-compositing.
4. No diffusion. Holes must stay marked or fall back to L1 with a mask.
5. Compare: L1, your alpha view-interp, exact Meta deghost softmax, and depth-ordered over.

If depth-ordered over produces one sharp object with only small holes, your 2D ceiling was self-inflicted. If it fails on confident geometry, stop spending time on seam/graphcut/alpha-blend variants; the remaining path is learned dense geometry/3DGS, not more 2D polish.
